  "body": "Dear WHATWG team,\r\nwhat is cool about SPAs is that there is no white screen when transitioning from one page to the next. This is not possible due to the progressive rendering style of the HTML.\r\n\r\n¿Why do HTML users need to be forced to use Javascript frameworks just to have a fluent UX?\r\n\r\n¿Could it be possible to define a new tag in HTML that tells the browser to render a page offscreen and then show it when a user defined event is called? By default could be document.onLoad.\r\n\r\nFor example: \r\n\r\n```\r\n<html>\r\n  <head>\r\n    <meta name=\"render-offsccreen\" content=\"onRender\"/> <!-- the event the page has to call to be shown, by default onLoad -->\r\n  </head>\r\n</head>\r\n<body>\r\n....\r\nRender offscreen \r\n....\r\n  <script>\r\n    onRender(); //Calls the browser to show the rendered webpage now.\r\n  </script>\r\n</body>\r\n</html>\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\nThanks for your attention.\r\nPere Joseph Rodríguez\r\n",
